  { w:"helpmeet", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"From Genesis 2:18 in the King James Bible, where God promises Adam ‘an help meet for him’, meaning ‘a helper suitable for him’. Readers ran ‘help meet’ together as a noun meaning wife, and it was further altered by folk etymology to ‘helpmate’.",
    from:"English", year:1670 },
  { w:"buttonhole", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"The noun, a hole for a button, is straightforward. The verb ‘to buttonhole’, to detain someone in conversation, comes from an earlier ‘button-hold’, holding someone by a coat button, which was altered by association with the familiar noun in the 19th century.",
    from:"English", year:1560 },
  { w:"bridegroom", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"Old English <i>brȳdguma</i>, ‘bride’s man’, from <i>guma</i>, ‘man’, related to Latin <i>homo</i>. When <i>guma</i> fell out of use, speakers replaced it with the similar-sounding ‘groom’, then meaning a lad or servant.",
    from:"Old English", year:1000 },
  { w:"female", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"From Latin <i>femella</i>, ‘young woman’, a diminutive of <i>femina</i>, via Old French <i>femelle</i>. Its spelling was changed to match ‘male’, but the two words are unrelated: ‘male’ comes from Latin <i>masculus</i>.",
    from:"Latin", year:1300 },
  { w:"sirloin", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"From Old French <i>surlonge</i>, ‘above the loin’. The story that an English king, variously Henry VIII, James I or Charles II, knighted a joint of beef ‘Sir Loin’ is a jest; it was, however, probably responsible for the ‘sir-’ spelling that became established from the 18th century.",
    from:"French", year:1450 },
  { w:"crayfish", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"From Old French <i>crevice</i>, a crustacean (related to modern French <i>écrevisse</i>). English speakers reshaped the unfamiliar ending into ‘fish’, although the animal is not a fish.",
    from:"French", year:1350 },
  { w:"dord", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"A ‘ghost word’ that appeared in Webster’s New International Dictionary (second edition, 1934), defined as ‘density’ in physics and chemistry. It arose from a slip reading ‘D or d’, abbreviations for density, which was misfiled as a word. The error was noticed in 1939 and removed in later printings.",
    from:"English (error)", year:1934 },
  { w:"cockroach", group:"Words from mistakes & folk etymology",
    story:"From Spanish <i>cucaracha</i>. Captain John Smith recorded ‘cacarootch’ in 1624, and English speakers reshaped the strange word into two familiar ones, ‘cock’ and ‘roach’ (a fish), although the insect has nothing to do with either.",
    from:"Spanish", year:1624 }
